WEBVTT

1
00:00:02.900 --> 00:00:05.416
In part one we proved things

2
00:00:05.505 --> 00:00:07.033
by contradiction.

3
00:00:07.033 --> 00:00:10.087
Here is a question for that tool

4
00:00:10.177 --> 00:00:12.693
is there a smallest positive

5
00:00:12.783 --> 00:00:14.220
rational number?

6
00:00:14.570 --> 00:00:16.460
Start at one half.

7
00:00:16.460 --> 00:00:21.080
Half of it is one quarter, then one eighth

8
00:00:21.080 --> 00:00:24.650
one sixteenth, one thirty-second.

9
00:00:25.000 --> 00:00:27.774
The points crowd towards zero

10
00:00:27.870 --> 00:00:29.687
but never reach it.

11
00:00:29.687 --> 00:00:32.078
Is there a smallest one?

12
00:00:32.078 --> 00:00:33.800
Pause and decide.

13
00:00:37.380 --> 00:00:39.587
Suppose, to the contrary

14
00:00:39.587 --> 00:00:43.294
that r is the smallest positive rational.

15
00:00:43.294 --> 00:00:45.854
Then r over two is rational

16
00:00:45.854 --> 00:00:47.620
and it is positive.

17
00:00:47.970 --> 00:00:50.827
But r over two is less than r.

18
00:00:50.827 --> 00:00:55.017
A positive rational below the smallest one

19
00:00:55.017 --> 00:00:56.635
a contradiction.

20
00:00:56.635 --> 00:01:00.730
So there is no smallest positive rational.

21
00:01:01.080 --> 00:01:03.476
Positive numbers in general need

22
00:01:03.550 --> 00:01:05.347
not have a smallest one.

23
00:01:05.347 --> 00:01:07.518
Whatever candidate you pick

24
00:01:07.518 --> 00:01:09.240
half of it is smaller.

25
00:01:09.820 --> 00:01:12.467
The natural numbers are different.

26
00:01:12.467 --> 00:01:15.347
This is the well-ordering principle

27
00:01:15.425 --> 00:01:17.682
every nonempty set of natural

28
00:01:17.760 --> 00:01:19.940
numbers has a least element.

29
00:01:20.290 --> 00:01:23.983
Take the n whose square is more than fifty.

30
00:01:23.983 --> 00:01:27.418
Seven squared is forty-nine, too small.

31
00:01:27.418 --> 00:01:29.908
Eight squared is sixty-four.

32
00:01:29.908 --> 00:01:32.570
So the least element is eight.

33
00:01:32.920 --> 00:01:35.612
Or take the n, at least one

34
00:01:35.612 --> 00:01:39.939
where seven divides two to the n, minus one

35
00:01:39.939 --> 00:01:43.496
three, six, nine, twelve, and so on.

36
00:01:43.496 --> 00:01:45.419
The least is three

37
00:01:45.419 --> 00:01:48.880
since two cubed minus one is seven.

38
00:01:49.230 --> 00:01:50.606
Both words matter.

39
00:01:50.606 --> 00:01:53.357
The integers have no least element

40
00:01:53.357 --> 00:01:56.337
and neither do the positive rationals.

41
00:01:56.337 --> 00:01:59.470
And the empty set has no element at all.

42
00:02:00.050 --> 00:02:02.156
Now use it to prove things.

43
00:02:02.234 --> 00:02:04.496
Suppose a claim about natural

44
00:02:04.574 --> 00:02:06.446
numbers fails somewhere.

45
00:02:06.524 --> 00:02:08.942
The numbers where it fails form

46
00:02:09.020 --> 00:02:10.970
a set of counterexamples.

47
00:02:11.320 --> 00:02:13.339
That set is not empty

48
00:02:13.339 --> 00:02:16.092
so it has a least element, m.

49
00:02:16.092 --> 00:02:18.478
Everything below m works.

50
00:02:18.478 --> 00:02:21.047
Then show that m works too

51
00:02:21.047 --> 00:02:23.800
and you have a contradiction.

52
00:02:24.380 --> 00:02:29.269
Theorem: three divides n cubed plus two n

53
00:02:29.269 --> 00:02:32.528
for every natural number n.

54
00:02:32.528 --> 00:02:36.719
The values are zero, three, twelve

55
00:02:36.719 --> 00:02:39.862
thirty-three, seventy-two

56
00:02:39.862 --> 00:02:42.656
all multiples of three.

57
00:02:42.656 --> 00:02:45.100
But we want every n.

58
00:02:45.450 --> 00:02:49.757
Suppose, to the contrary, that some n fails

59
00:02:49.757 --> 00:02:53.574
and let m be the least counterexample.

60
00:02:53.574 --> 00:02:55.042
m is not zero

61
00:02:55.042 --> 00:02:58.370
because zero is three times zero.

62
00:02:58.720 --> 00:03:03.625
So m minus one is a natural number below m

63
00:03:03.625 --> 00:03:07.503
and it works: m minus one, cubed

64
00:03:07.503 --> 00:03:10.697
plus two times m minus one

65
00:03:10.697 --> 00:03:13.320
is three times some q.

66
00:03:13.670 --> 00:03:18.119
Now m cubed plus two m is that number plus

67
00:03:18.225 --> 00:03:22.357
three times m squared minus m plus one.

68
00:03:22.357 --> 00:03:26.064
Both parts are multiples of three.

69
00:03:26.064 --> 00:03:30.196
So m works after all: a contradiction.

70
00:03:30.196 --> 00:03:32.950
No counterexample exists.

71
00:03:33.530 --> 00:03:35.235
Now a warning.

72
00:03:35.235 --> 00:03:40.593
Claim: two to the n is at least n plus two

73
00:03:40.593 --> 00:03:44.003
for every natural number n.

74
00:03:44.003 --> 00:03:48.266
Let m be the least counterexample.

75
00:03:48.266 --> 00:03:51.188
Then m minus one works

76
00:03:51.188 --> 00:03:55.086
and two to the m is twice that

77
00:03:55.086 --> 00:03:58.130
at least two m plus two.

78
00:03:58.480 --> 00:04:00.321
Every step looks fine.

79
00:04:00.321 --> 00:04:03.502
But test the claim: it fails at zero

80
00:04:03.502 --> 00:04:06.849
since one is less than two, and at one

81
00:04:06.849 --> 00:04:09.360
since two is less than three.

82
00:04:09.710 --> 00:04:12.566
The least counterexample is zero

83
00:04:12.652 --> 00:04:14.989
and zero has no m minus one

84
00:04:15.076 --> 00:04:17.326
among the natural numbers.

85
00:04:17.326 --> 00:04:20.355
The proof never checked the start.

86
00:04:20.355 --> 00:04:23.470
The claim is true only from two on.

87
00:04:24.050 --> 00:04:27.617
So a proof by smallest counterexample

88
00:04:27.713 --> 00:04:29.159
has five steps.

89
00:04:29.159 --> 00:04:32.051
One, suppose the claim fails.

90
00:04:32.051 --> 00:04:35.810
Two, take the least counterexample, m.

91
00:04:36.160 --> 00:04:39.447
Three, everything below m works

92
00:04:39.447 --> 00:04:43.965
check the start before you use m minus one.

93
00:04:43.965 --> 00:04:46.944
Four, reach a contradiction.

94
00:04:46.944 --> 00:04:51.360
Five, conclude that the claim never fails.

95
00:04:51.940 --> 00:04:53.392
Try it yourself

96
00:04:53.392 --> 00:04:57.022
prove that five divides n to the fifth

97
00:04:57.022 --> 00:05:00.380
minus n, for every natural number n.

98
00:05:00.730 --> 00:05:02.373
Check zero first

99
00:05:02.373 --> 00:05:05.467
then get from m minus one to m.

100
00:05:05.467 --> 00:05:09.333
That shape has its own name: induction

101
00:05:09.333 --> 00:05:12.330
the topic of the next lecture.
